{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794177013,"nonce":"MiPAELB24P7Njn+t7tfFHL8iMr4=","message":"What is built adds two things to the approved design, both needed for 'export | import remains a no-op' to hold on the default export:\n\n1. **Reconcile keeps an archived value a field's entry leaves out.** Reconcile owns a field's values, so before this an enum value missing from the document was removed: importing the default export would have deleted every archived value (its ordinal, category and name with it). Now an archived value the document omits is kept as it stands, as an omitted entity is. A live value omitted is still removed. Recorded in doc/design/schema.md, \"Import is an upsert\" and \"Export hides what is archived\"; doc/design/jira.md's \"Reconcile owns every value\" now says every live value.\n2. **Import validation knows the store's archived types.** It used the live type keys only, so a live field targeting an archived type (or a retype target naming one) was refused once the default export left that type out. It now passes every type key, archived included, matching the compiled schema, which keeps an archived type.\n\nInternal readers take the whole export: the Jira derive (host.SchemaExport(repo, true)) and `$schema` (an issue on an archived value still sorts by its ordinal and category), so `$schema` is documented as `schema export --include-archive --format json`. New heading: doc/design/schema.md, \"Export hides what is archived\".","files":null}]}